# tools/al/ales_stereo_zero_cross_witness.rish -- stereo_zero_cross: the zero-crossing counter carried into stereo with ONE SHARED BAND and TWO INDEPENDENT COUNTERS, the suite's first stereo analysis rung over the hysteresis comparator (ALES145).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES145.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-044940_fill-ales145-lotus-stereo-zero-cross.md
# Stands on: lotus/zero_cross.rye (ALES93 -- the mono zero-crossing counter run once per channel, its from-silence count_crossings, its carried ZeroCounter, its ZeroCrossError, all reused whole), lotus/schmitt.rye (ALES92 --
# the hysteresis comparator whose flips these count through ALES93, and its precheck band-and-span law), lotus/stereo_meter.rye (ALES17 -- the stereo analysis shape this mirrors, two mono readers with per-channel accessors),
# lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that
# left and right hold the same length. Where ALES144 carried the Schmitt trigger's WRITE into stereo, this rung carries its READ: two counters reading two channels, writing not one sample. A zero crossing in the hysteresis
# sense is a rail flip, so counting flips reads how many times each channel genuinely crossed past the noise the band suppresses -- the oldest activity / pitch estimate there is. Each channel carries its own counter (rail,
# count, previous-rail memory), so the two counts are independent -- an identical-channel master reading equal counts, a busier channel counting more -- and a two-piece StereoZeroCounter count equals the whole counted once on
# both channels, each seam flip counted exactly once, ALES93's carried-state crux made stereo.
#
# lotus/stereo_zero_cross.rye validates the shared band and length once against ALES93's own precheck before either channel is read (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span outside the samples, CountFull on the
# accumulation bound), then counts both channels through ALES93's counter; ZeroCrossError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO ZERO-CROSS LAW: each channel's count equals ALES93's mono count_crossings with the same
# band. THE READ-ONLY / BALANCE LAW: the source StereoClip byte for byte unchanged on both, the pair balanced. THE INDEPENDENT-COUNTER LAW: an identical master reading equal counts, a busier channel counting more, chatter
# suppressed on each, in-band-only zero on both. THE CARRIED-STATE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a two-piece count equals the whole once on both (each seam flip counted once), a refusal leaves both counters and the clip
# untouched, count zero the identity.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_zero_cross_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-zero-cross: Language EN — stereo_zero_cross counts the rail flips over a span in both channels of a stereo master with the same band, returning the pair of per-channel counts and writing not one sample. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. This rung carries the suite's first analysis rung into stereo on ALES93's zero-crossing counter, running the proven mono counter on each channel with the same band, validated once against the shared band and length before either channel is read so a refusal never counts one channel and leaves the other's state moved."
say "ales-stereo-zero-cross: Style Radiant — where ALES144 carried the Schmitt trigger's write into stereo, two rails writing two clips, this rung carries its read: two counters reading two channels. A zero crossing in the hysteresis sense is a rail flip, so counting flips reads how many times each channel genuinely crossed past the noise the band already suppresses, the oldest activity and pitch estimate there is, the front of voiced and unvoiced detection and the cheapest pitch tracker. It mirrors the stereo meter's own shape, two mono readers with per-channel accessors, and offers ALES93's two forms mirrored: count_crossings_stereo counts each channel from silence and returns the pair, and StereoZeroCounter holds each channel's own counter so a keeper feeds span after span and reads a per-channel running count. The honest signature of a stereo analysis rung is that each channel carries its own counter, its own trigger rail, its own running count, its own previous-rail memory, so the two counts advance independently: an identical-channel master gives equal counts, a mono-in-stereo signal reading the same on both, while a busy left over a calm right reports more crossings on the left. The crux is split-equals-whole carried up one level, in stereo: a flip can land exactly at the seam between two counted pieces, so the counter carries each channel's rail and previous-rail memory across a call, and a stereo span counted in two pieces, each channel's second piece continuing that channel's state, equals the whole counted once on both channels, each seam flip counted exactly once, never missed and never doubled, ALES93's crux made stereo. The rule is stated positively: the counter reads only, the source clip leaving byte for byte unchanged on both channels, and the count is a pure sample count of rail flips, not a pitch in Hz, since there is no real sample rate in the suite yet, so it reports the raw honest count per channel and names the pitch conversion a later rung. It reuses ALES93's ZeroCrossError by name, ALES93's counter for the arithmetic, and ALES92's precheck for the band-and-span law, inventing no new fault and no new primitive. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo zero-cross law, the read-only / balance law, the independent-counter law (an identical master reading equal counts, a busier channel counting more, chatter suppressed on each, in-band-only zero on both), and the carried-state / atomicity / degenerate law (a two-piece count equalling the whole once on both, any refusal leaving both counters and the clip untouched, count zero the identity)."
say "ales-stereo-zero-cross: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) read in lockstep by one shared band, each carrying its own counter (rail, count, previous-rail memory, started bit); the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the shared band and span are bound-checked once via ALES92's precheck before any read, and because those checks hold identically for both balanced channels each mono counter is pre-validated to succeed so no refusal can land between the two channel counts or move a single counter; the counter composes ALES93's ZeroCounter.feed per channel, which composes ALES92's public one-sample comparator and reads its flips without writing a sample, the accumulation bounded by max_crossings far below the u32 top so the guard fires long before any wrap; StereoZeroCounter shares its band through a single init so left and right can never drift to different thresholds, threads each channel's carried rail and previous-rail memory, and resets both counters together; the stereo zero-cross law is proven against ALES93's own count_crossings run per channel, the independent-counter law proves an identical master reads equal counts while a busy channel counts more and a calm one counts zero, the carried-state crux proves a two-piece count equals the whole once on both channels, the read-only law proves the source clip is byte-for-byte unchanged on both, and the atomicity cases leave both counters and the clip untouched with count zero the identity; siloed to lotus/, read-only, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_zero_cross"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_zero_cross.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-zero-cross: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_zero_cross.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-zero-cross: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-zero-cross: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-zero-cross' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-zero-cross: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-zero-cross line"
